text:
Unification Does Not Require One Universal Object
This changes the usual unification problem.
A common strategy is to search for one final object from which every other scientific object is built.
R2D proposes a different invariant.
Not:
\[
\boxed{
\text{one object at every scale}.
}
\]
But:
\[
\boxed{
\text{one formal architecture by which objects become distinguishable}.
}
\]
That architecture is
\[
\boxed{
\text{boundary}
\rightarrow
\text{distinction}
\rightarrow
\text{classification}
\rightarrow
\text{multiplicity}
\rightarrow
\text{realization}.
}
\]
The objects change.
The grammar need not.

Boundary Relative Does Not Mean Observer Arbitrary
A boundary is also not merely whatever an observer chooses to imagine.
The proposed boundary must correctly identify:

the readable distinctions;
the compatible realization domain;
the macrostate classification;
the multiplicity structure;
the observable transitions;
and the enclosing relations organizing realization.

A poor boundary definition will fail empirically.
Boundary relativity therefore does not mean arbitrary relativity.
It means that scientific claims must be indexed to the count domain in which their objects are defined.

The Primitive Invariant Is Not the Object
This leads to the broadest interpretation of R2D.
The proposed invariant across science is not:

one particle;
one molecule;
one force;
one field;
one organism;
one geometry;
or one universal ruler.

It is the architecture by which scientific objects become defined.
That architecture includes:
\[
\boxed{
\text{boundary}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\text{distinguishability}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\text{classification}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\text{multiplicity}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\text{occurrence}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\text{occupancy}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\text{recurrence}
}
\]
and
\[
\boxed{
\text{recursive replacement}.
}
\]
The object can change.
The architecture can remain.

The Next Boundary Counts Landscapes, Not Restored Coins
Now suppose the next boundary contains ten such ensemble boundaries.
The crucial point is that \(B_{n+1}\) does not return to the lower description and treat one hundred individual \(H/T\) identities as its primitive states.
It counts ten already-defined \(B_n\) landscapes.
Schematically:
\[
\boxed{
\underbrace{
B_n,\,
B_n,\,
\dots,\,
B_n
}_{10\ \text{peer landscapes}}
\rightarrow
B_{n+1}.
}
\]
A closed-form calculation may ultimately reproduce a familiar combinatorial result associated with one hundred binary elements.
But that mathematical equivalence does not restore the one hundred lower-boundary identities as the primitive state variables at \(B_{n+1}\).
This is the distinction between:
\[
\boxed{
\text{recursive count consequence}
}
\]
and
\[
\boxed{
\text{persistence of subordinate state identity}.
}
\]
They are not the same claim.

Recursion Is Not Bottom-Up Causation
The direction of counting must not be confused with the direction of causal organization.
Recursive carry can be written
\[
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\xmapsto{\kappa_n}
\chi_{n+1}^{(r)}.
\]
This looks upward.
But the arrow means reclassification, not causation.
Causally, the enclosing occurrence defines which local realizations are compatible with it:
\[
\boxed{
\chi_{n+1}^{(r)}
\Longrightarrow
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}.
}
\]
Thus two complementary relations coexist:
\[
\boxed{
\text{enclosing organization}
\Longrightarrow
\text{local realization}
}
\]
and
\[
\boxed{
\text{completed local realization}
\xmapsto{\text{boundary change}}
\text{enclosing count meaning}.
}
\]
Recursive counting therefore does not require smaller things to build larger things causally.
The Causal Inversion →

Recurrence Supplies the Across-Boundary Count
Once a local closure is complete, it can recur.
Within a common recurrence-comparison domain \(\mathcal D_R\), let
\[
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\right)
\]
count completed local closure recurrences.
Let
\[
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\chi_{n+1}
\right)
\]
count corresponding enclosing recurrences.
The adjacent recurrence ratio is
\[
\boxed{
\rho_{n+1|n,\alpha}
=
\frac{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\chi_{n+1}
\right)
}{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\right)
}.
}
\]
This ratio tells us how recursive realization scales from one boundary to the next.
Recurrence →

Logarithms Turn Recursive Scaling Into Addition
Define the adjacent logarithmic recurrence scaling
\[
\gamma_{j+1|j}
=
\ln\rho_{j+1|j}.
\]
Then the accumulated recurrence scaling from \(B_n\) to \(B_N\) is
\[
\boxed{
\Gamma_{N|n}
=
\sum_{j=n}^{N-1}
\ln\rho_{j+1|j}.
}
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\boxed{
\Gamma_{N|n}
=
\ln\rho_{N|n}.
}
\]
Again, multiplicative recursive composition becomes additive in logarithmic coordinates.
This is the same mathematical architecture already encountered with multiplicity.

Distinguishability Also Scales Across Boundaries
Recurrence is only one aspect of the recursive relation.
A readable transition at boundary \(B_j\) also has a distinguishable interval
\[
\lambda_j.
\]
For a reference boundary \(B_n\) and a larger boundary \(B_N\), define the accumulated distinguishability scaling
\[
\boxed{
\Lambda_{N|n}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
|\lambda_N|
}{
|\lambda_n|
}
\right).
}
\]
This asks how the readable distinguishable interval changes across the same recursive hierarchy.
Thus recursive comparison contains two independent scale relations:
\[
\boxed{
\Lambda
=
\text{distinguishability scaling}
}
\]
and
\[
\boxed{
\Gamma
=
\text{recurrence scaling}.
}
\]
Neither is yet space or time.

Two Coordinates of Recursive Readability
This gives R2D two primitive aspects of recursive closure.
One asks:

How does distinguishability change across boundaries?

The other asks:

How does recurrence change across boundaries?

These are quantified by
\[
\Lambda_{N|n}
\]
and
\[
\Gamma_{N|n}.
\]
Their accumulated difference can be written
\[
\boxed{
H_{N|n}
=
\Lambda_{N|n}
-
\Gamma_{N|n}.
}
\]
or
\[
\boxed{
H_{N|n}
=
\ln
\left[
\frac{
|\lambda_N|/|\lambda_n|
}{
\displaystyle
\prod_{j=n}^{N-1}
\rho_{j+1|j}
}
\right].
}
\]
This quantity is not a third primitive coordinate.
It measures the relative accumulated scaling of the two.

Core Equations

Recursive carry
\[
\boxed{
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\xmapsto{\kappa_n}
\chi_{n+1}^{(r)}
}
\]


Adjacent recurrence ratio
\[
\boxed{
\rho_{n+1|n,\alpha}
=
\frac{
N_{\mathcal D_R}(\chi_{n+1})
}{
N_{\mathcal D_R}(\mathcal C_{n,\alpha})
}
}
\]


Accumulated recurrence scaling
\[
\boxed{
\Gamma_{N|n}
=
\sum_{j=n}^{N-1}
\ln\rho_{j+1|j}
}
\]


Accumulated distinguishability scaling
\[
\boxed{
\Lambda_{N|n}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
|\lambda_N|
}{
|\lambda_n|
}
\right)
}
\]


Relative accumulated scaling
\[
\boxed{
H_{N|n}
=
\Lambda_{N|n}
-
\Gamma_{N|n}
}
\]

The Adjacent Recurrence Ratio
For
\[
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\right)&gt;0,
\]
define
\[
\boxed{
\rho_{n+1|n,\alpha}
=
\frac{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\chi_{n+1}
\right)
}{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\right)
}.
}
\]
The ratio asks:

How many enclosing recurrences are realized per completed recurrence of the representative local closure?

This is one of the central across-boundary count relations in R2D.

Recurrence Is Different From Reciprocal Realization
Recall the closure efficiency
\[
\eta_{n,\alpha,\mathcal C}
=
\frac{
\nu_{n,\alpha,L}
}{
\nu_{n,\alpha,H}
}.
\]
This asks whether the two opposed closure legs are realized with equal occurrence-count efficiency.
The recurrence ratio
\[
\rho_{n+1|n,\alpha}
\]
asks something completely different.
It compares the number of completed recurrences across adjacent boundaries.
Thus:
\[
\boxed{
\eta
\neq
\rho.
}
\]
A closure can have reciprocal opposed-leg realization
\[
\eta=1
\]
while recurring at a very different count from its enclosing transition.
Likewise, nonreciprocal occurrence realization does not itself tell us how often the whole closure recurs.

Core Equations

Whole-closure recurrence count
\[
\boxed{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\right)
}
\]


Enclosing recurrence count
\[
\boxed{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\chi_{n+1}
\right)
}
\]


Adjacent recurrence ratio
\[
\boxed{
\rho_{n+1|n,\alpha}
=
\frac{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\chi_{n+1}
\right)
}{
N_{\mathcal D_R}
\left(
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\right)
}
}
\]


Logarithmic recurrence scaling
\[
\boxed{
\gamma_{n+1|n,\alpha}
=
\ln
\rho_{n+1|n,\alpha}
}
\]


Recursive composition
\[
\boxed{
\rho_{N|n}
=
\prod_{j=n}^{N-1}
\rho_{j+1|j}
}
\]
and therefore
\[
\boxed{
\ln\rho_{N|n}
=
\sum_{j=n}^{N-1}
\ln\rho_{j+1|j}.
}
\]

Extension Per Local Possibility
A local macrostate with large multiplicity naturally contains more local realizations.
So simply counting the total number of compatible enclosing realizations is not yet enough.
R2D therefore defines the mean compatible enclosing extension multiplicity per local possibility:
\[
\boxed{
K_{n+1|n,i}
=
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
}
\]
This asks:

For each local possibility classified as \(i\), how much compatible enclosing realization is available?

The quantity \(K\) is still a count ratio.
No energy, force, probability, or physical potential has yet been introduced.

Possibility Is the Logarithm of Compatible Extension
R2D defines possibility as
\[
\boxed{
P_{n,i}
=
\ln K_{n+1|n,i}
}
\]
or equivalently,
\[
\boxed{
P_{n,i}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
\right).
}
\]
Because
\[
S_{n,i}
=
\ln W_{n,i},
\]
and the compatible enclosing entropy is
\[
S^{\mathrm{comp}}_{n+1|n,i}
=
\ln \Theta_{n+1|n,i},
\]
possibility can also be written
\[
\boxed{
P_{n,i}
=
S^{\mathrm{comp}}_{n+1|n,i}
-
S_{n,i}.
}
\]
Possibility is therefore an across-boundary difference in logarithmic possible count.

Directional Possibility
For a readable local transition
\[
i \rightarrow k,
\]
define the possibility difference
\[
\boxed{
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
P_{n,k}
-
P_{n,i}.
}
\]
Substituting the compatible extension multiplicities gives
\[
\boxed{
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
K_{n+1|n,k}
}{
K_{n+1|n,i}
}
\right).
}
\]
Thus:

if \(K_{n+1|n,k} &gt; K_{n+1|n,i}\), then \(\Delta P&gt;0\);
if \(K_{n+1|n,k} = K_{n+1|n,i}\), then \(\Delta P=0\);
if \(K_{n+1|n,k} &lt; K_{n+1|n,i}\), then \(\Delta P&lt;0\).

Direction emerges from unequal compatible enclosing extension.

The Local and Enclosing Counts Fit Together
For the transition
\[
i \rightarrow k,
\]
the directional possibility difference can be expanded as
\[
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,k}
}{
W_{n,k}
}
\right)
-
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
\right).
\]
Rearranging gives
\[
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,k}
}{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}
\right)
-
\ln
\left(
\frac{
W_{n,k}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
\right).
\]
Since
\[
\Delta S_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
W_{n,k}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
\right),
\]
we obtain
\[
\boxed{
\Delta S_{n,i\rightarrow k}
+
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,k}
}{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}
\right).
}
\]
This equation makes the architecture transparent.
The left side separates:

native local multiplicity difference, and
across-boundary possibility difference.

Together they equal the difference in enclosing multiplicity compatible with the two local alternatives.

The Architecture So Far
The count architecture can now be extended:
\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{boundary}
\\
\Downarrow
\\
\text{local multiplicity}
\\
\Downarrow
\\
\text{possible local structure}
\end{array}
}
\]
while across the enclosing relation,
\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{compatible enclosing multiplicity}
\\
\Downarrow
\\
P
\\
\Downarrow
\\
\Delta P
\\
\Downarrow
\\
\text{directional support}
\end{array}
}
\]
Multiplicity structures what can occur locally.
Possibility distinguishes how those local alternatives participate in the enclosing whole.

Core Equations

Local multiplicity
\[
\boxed{
W_{n,i}
=
\left|
\pi_n^{-1}(i)
\right|
}
\]


Compatible enclosing multiplicity
\[
\boxed{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
=
\left|
\mathcal{E}_{n+1|n,i}
\right|
}
\]


Enclosing extension per local possibility
\[
\boxed{
K_{n+1|n,i}
=
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
}
\]


Possibility
\[
\boxed{
P_{n,i}
=
\ln K_{n+1|n,i}
}
\]
or
\[
\boxed{
P_{n,i}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}{
W_{n,i}
}
\right)
}
\]


Directional possibility
\[
\boxed{
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
K_{n+1|n,k}
}{
K_{n+1|n,i}
}
\right)
}
\]


Local and enclosing count relation
\[
\boxed{
\Delta S_{n,i\rightarrow k}
+
\Delta P_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,k}
}{
\Theta_{n+1|n,i}
}
\right)
}
\]

A Model Can Be Effective Without Being Ontologically Primitive
This gives R2D a way to preserve successful science.
A model can:

predict observations;
identify useful effective variables;
compress inaccessible detail;
and correctly describe correlations

without proving that its state variables are the primitive objects of nature.
That distinction is especially important across scientific scales.
Predictive success and primitive ontology are different questions.

The R2D Difference in One Diagram
The ordinary coarse-graining picture can be represented as
\[
\boxed{
\text{underlying detailed state}
\rightarrow
\text{reduced representation of that state}.
}
\]
The R2D architecture is
\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{state domain at }B_n
\\[4pt]
\Downarrow
\\[4pt]
\text{completed realization}
\\[4pt]
\Downarrow
\\[4pt]
\text{change of count boundary}
\\[4pt]
\Downarrow
\\[4pt]
\text{new state domain at }B_{n+1}
\end{array}
}
\]
What survives is not necessarily an unchanged lower-boundary state.
What survives is the recursively organized relation.

Core Relations

Boundary-relative classification
\[
\boxed{
\pi_n:
\mathfrak M_n
\rightarrow
\mathcal M_n
}
\]


Boundary change
\[
\boxed{
B_n
\rightarrow
B_{n+1}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\mathcal M_n
\not\equiv
\mathcal M_{n+1}
}
\]


Change in transition meaning
\[
\boxed{
\text{macrostate transition at }B_{n-1,\alpha}
=
\text{microstate transition relative to }B_n
}
\]


Recursive replacement
\[
\boxed{
\text{state identity changes while recursive consequence can remain}
}
\]


Recursive carry
\[
\boxed{
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\xmapsto{\kappa_n}
\chi_{n+1}^{(r)}
}
\]


Distinct boundary-specific objects
\[
\boxed{
\mathcal C_{n,\alpha}
\not\equiv
\chi_{n+1}^{(r)}
}
\]

Statehood Comes Before Physical Ontology
Nothing in the formal definitions requires the peer boundaries to be:

molecules;
atoms;
particles;
spins;
proteins;
cells;
coins;
or spatial regions.

Those physical interpretations come later.
The primitive structure is:
\[
\boxed{
\text{boundary}
\rightarrow
\text{compatible joint realization}
\rightarrow
\text{classification}
\rightarrow
\text{readable state}
}
\]
Only afterward does science assign a physical interpretation to that state.
This is how R2D mathematics operates across scientific domains.
The physical object changes.
The count grammar remains the same.

The Logical Fulcrum
The entire R2D program can therefore be challenged at a very specific point.
Ask:

Are these definitions of macrostate and microstate wrong?

If they are wrong, the architecture built upon them fails.
If they are accepted, several later features of R2D follow naturally:

boundary-relative objecthood;
recursive replacement;
the distinction between possible structure and realization;
the primitive observer effect;
the many-body readability problem;
recursive carry;
and eventually the complementary readability horizons.

The definitions are therefore not merely terminology.
They determine the architecture that follows.

R2D uses familiar mathematical words in deliberately specific ways.
In particular:

a boundary defines a count domain;
a macrostate is a readable state at that boundary;
a microstate is one compatible joint realization of immediately subordinate boundaries;
multiplicity, occurrence, and occupancy are different kinds of count;
possibility is not probability;
recurrence is not occurrence;
closure is not automatically reversibility;
and primitive R2D quantities are dimensionless before physical mappings are assigned.

This page is a reference.
For the conceptual development, begin with Counting Requires Distinguishability →.


Every R2D quantity must be interpreted relative to the boundary at which it is defined.
Thus:

Always ask: At which boundary is this quantity readable?

The same physical realization can have different count meanings at adjacent boundaries.
That boundary index is part of the definition.

(B_n) — Count boundary
\[
\boxed{
B_n
}
\]
is the count boundary at recursive level (n).
A boundary is not necessarily a physical surface.
It defines:

the count domain;
the readable distinctions;
which subordinate realizations are compatible;
how those realizations are classified into macrostates;
and which completed local realizations belong to enclosing occurrences.

Boundaries Define Scientific Objects →

Joint subordinate realization space
The unrestricted joint realization space subordinate to (B_n) is
\[
\boxed{
\prod_{\alpha\in I_{n-1}}
\mathfrak X_{n-1,\alpha}.
}
\]
This contains all formally possible combinations of subordinate peer realizations.
The enclosing boundary need not admit all of them.

(S_n^{}) — Total logarithmic multiplicity
\[
\boxed{
S_n^{\mathrm{tot}}
=
\ln\Theta_n.
}
\]

(E_{n+1|n,,i}) — Enclosing compatibility fiber
For local peer () classified as state (i),
\[
\boxed{
\mathcal E_{n+1|n,\alpha,i}
}
\]
is the set of enclosing formal microstates compatible with that local classification.

(K_{n+1|n,,i}) — Compatible enclosing extension per local possibility
Define
\[
\boxed{
K_{n+1|n,\alpha,i}
=
\frac{
\Theta_{n+1|n,\alpha,i}
}{
W_{n,\alpha,i}
}.
}
\]
This gives the compatible enclosing extension multiplicity per local possibility.

(P_{n,,ik}) — Directional possibility
\[
\boxed{
\Delta P_{n,\alpha,i\rightarrow k}
=
P_{n,\alpha,k}
-
P_{n,\alpha,i}.
}
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\boxed{
\Delta P_{n,\alpha,i\rightarrow k}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{
K_{n+1|n,\alpha,k}
}{
K_{n+1|n,\alpha,i}
}
\right).
}
\]
If
\[
K_{n+1|n,\alpha,k}
=
K_{n+1|n,\alpha,i},
\]
then
\[
\boxed{
\Delta P=0.
}
\]
Large absolute possibility does not by itself create direction.
Unequal enclosing compatibility does.
Possibility →

(_{n,i}) — State occurrence count
\[
\boxed{
\nu_{n,i}
}
\]
is the number of realized formal microstate occurrences classified as macrostate (i) within a specified finite realization domain.
Occurrence is realized instance count.

(_{n,ik}) — State occurrence difference
\[
\boxed{
\Delta\tau_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
\tau_{n,k}-\tau_{n,i}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{\nu_{n,k}}{\nu_{n,i}}
\right).
}
\]

(c_{n,i}) — Occupancy
\[
\boxed{
c_{n,i}
}
\]
is the realized occupancy associated with macrostate (i).
Occupancy describes realized structure across the macrostate landscape.
It is not the same as the number of occurrences.

(G_{n,i}) — Logarithmic occupancy coordinate
For positive occupancy,
\[
\boxed{
G_{n,i}
=
\ln c_{n,i}.
}
\]
This (G) is not Gibbs free energy.

(G_{n,ik}) — Occupancy difference
\[
\boxed{
\Delta G_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
G_{n,k}-G_{n,i}
=
\ln
\left(
\frac{c_{n,k}}{c_{n,i}}
\right).
}
\]

(i_n^*) — Structural macrostate
The structural macrostate is the macrostate of maximum occupancy:
\[
\boxed{
i_n^*
=
\operatorname*{arg\,max}_{i\in\mathcal M_n}
c_{n,i}.
}
\]

(_{n,ik}) — Readable distinguishability interval
\[
\boxed{
\lambda_{n,i\rightarrow k}
}
\]
is the oriented readable distinction between macrostates (i) and (k).
It reverses sign under reversal:
\[
\boxed{
\lambda_{n,k\rightarrow i}
=
-
\lambda_{n,i\rightarrow k}.
}
\]
At the primitive level, () is not necessarily spatial length, displacement, energy, or time.
Those are possible later physical reads.

(C_{n+1}) — Total enclosing constraint
\[
\boxed{
C_{n+1}\geq0
}
\]
is the total constraint associated with the enclosing occurrence.
It can be decomposed as
\[
\boxed{
C_{n+1}
=
C_{n+1}^{\mathrm{out}}
+
C_{n+1}^{\Phi}.
}
\]

(C_{n+1}^{}) — Productive/output constraint
\[
\boxed{
C_{n+1}^{\mathrm{out}}
}
\]
is the component that defines local retention and remains readable as productive distinguishable output.

(C_{n+1}^{}) — Background-associated constraint
\[
\boxed{
C_{n+1}^{\Phi}
}
\]
is the background-associated or dissipative component.
It contributes to the total enclosing constrained read but does not enter local retention.

(R_{n,ik}) — Local retention
For readable transition
\[
i\rightarrow k,
\]
retention is
\[
\boxed{
\Delta R_{n,i\rightarrow k}
=
C_{n+1}^{\mathrm{out}}
\lambda_{n,i\rightarrow k}.
}
\]
Because constraint is nonnegative and distinguishability is oriented,
\[
\boxed{
\Delta R_{n,k\rightarrow i}
=
-
\Delta R_{n,i\rightarrow k}
}
\]
when the same productive constraint applies.
Constraint and Retention →

(A_{n,ik}) — Net local asymmetry
Local asymmetry is the directional possibility remaining after retention:
\[
\boxed{
