The Core Distinction
The entire idea can be summarized in three statements:

Recursive replacement: the state domain changes.


Causal realization: the enclosing relation defines its compatible local realizations.


Recursive carry: a completed local realization acquires a new count meaning when the boundary changes.

Or schematically:
\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ccc}
& \chi_{n+1}^{(r)} & \\
& \Downarrow & \\
& \mathcal{C}_{n,\alpha} &
\end{array}
}
\]
for causal realization, while
\[
\boxed{
\mathcal{C}_{n,\alpha}
\xmapsto{\kappa_n}
\chi_{n+1}^{(r)}
}
\]
denotes reclassification.
The first is causal.
The second is not.
That distinction is essential to R2D.

Replacement Is Recursive
There is no reason for the process to stop after one boundary change.
Suppose the hierarchy is
\[
B_{n-1}
\rightarrow
B_n
\rightarrow
B_{n+1}
\rightarrow
B_{n+2}.
\]
At every step:

a boundary defines its own readable states;
those states are realized through compatible subordinate relations;
changing to the enclosing boundary changes the state domain;
the subordinate state identity is replaced by a new enclosing classification.

Thus:
\[
\boxed{
\text{replacement}
\rightarrow
\text{replacement}
\rightarrow
\text{replacement}
\rightarrow \cdots
}
\]
This is why the framework is called Recursively Realized Distinguishability.
The architecture repeats across boundaries.

Replacement and Carry Are Related but Distinct
Recursive replacement describes the change of state domain itself.
A state readable at one boundary is not preserved as the same state when the boundary changes.
Recursive carry describes how a completed realization at one boundary is reclassified from the enclosing boundary.
Thus:
\[
\boxed{
\text{replacement}
=
\text{change in state domain}
}
\]
whereas
\[
\boxed{
\text{carry}
=
\text{change in count meaning of a completed realization}.
}
\]
Keeping these concepts separate prevents an important mistake.
Nothing has to be physically transported upward from one scale to another.
The relation is a change of classification.

The Architecture So Far
